# TLS terminator for the self-hosted stack (SIGMA-266).
#
# The stack used to publish 8080 (control plane) and 3000 (dashboard) in
# plaintext and ship nothing in front of them, while .env.example insisted that
# SIGMAHUB_CP_PUBLIC_URL "MUST be https" — because the install command pipes
# /install.sh into `sudo bash`, and that script is the one artifact cosign
# cannot cover (it is what RUNS cosign). So the documented configuration named a
# URL nothing was listening on, and the only way to make the runbook work was to
# name the http one, which removes the product's stated trust boundary. This
# file is the missing half: the https name in .env now answers.
#
# The site addresses are the SAME two settings the rest of the stack already
# uses, rather than a third and fourth spelling of the same hostnames — Caddy
# accepts a full `https://host` as a site address, so SIGMAHUB_CP_PUBLIC_URL and
# WEB_PUBLIC_URL are read here verbatim. Set them to https names and Caddy
# provisions certificates over ACME on first request; set them to an http name
# (an IP-only staging box, say) and Caddy serves that plaintext instead, which
# the control plane then refuses to serve /install.sh over — see
# installerRelease in cp/internal/api/installer.go. One name, one behaviour, and
# the failure is loud on the side that can explain it.
{
	# ACME account contact. Caddy renews on its own, so this is only where the
	# CA sends expiry and policy notices; it is the same contact the control
	# plane uses for managed tenant domains.
	email {$CP_ACME_EMAIL:ops@example.com}
}

# The control plane. Hosts being onboarded fetch install.sh and every release
# asset from here, and agents long-poll it, so the proxy must not buffer or
# time out the stream: Caddy streams reverse_proxy responses by default and has
# no response timeout unless one is configured, which is what we want.
{$SIGMAHUB_CP_PUBLIC_URL:http://localhost} {
	reverse_proxy cp:8080
}

# The dashboard.
{$WEB_PUBLIC_URL:http://localhost:3000} {
	reverse_proxy web:3000
}
